   "answer": "Based on the lecture material provided, here is the explanation of branch divergence and its impact on utilization:\n\n**What is Branch Divergence?**\nBranch divergence occurs when threads within a single **warp** branch to different execution paths. The lecture notes that a GPU uses a **SIMD (Single Instruction, Multiple Data) pipeline** to save area on control logic, which groups scalar threads into warps [S1, p. 44]. Because the SIMD pipeline executes one instruction for the entire group at a time, if the threads in that group take different branches (e.g., one goes to an `if` block and another to an `else` block), they cannot all execute their specific path simultaneously in the same cycle [S1, p. 44].\n\n**Why does it reduce utilization?**\nIt reduces utilization because the result of branch divergence is that **only a subset of threads execute** at any given time, while the others are effectively idle or masked out. This leads to a **waste of SIMD pipeline utilization** [S1, p. 44].\n\n**Example from the Lecture:**\nThe lecture provides an example of intra-warp divergence where threads are split based on whether `threadIdx.x` is even or odd:\n```c\nCompute(threadIdx.x);\nif (threadIdx.x % 2 == 0){\n  Do_this(threadIdx.x);\n}\nelse{\n  Do_that(threadIdx.x);\n}\n```\nIn this case, half the threads in the warp execute `Do_this` while the other half execute `Do_that`, preventing the full warp from utilizing the pipeline efficiently for both operations simultaneously [S3, p. 45].\n\n**Solution Mentioned:**\nThe lecture suggests increasing SIMD utilization by writing **divergence-free programs**, where all threads in a warp take the same branch (e.g., branching based on `threadIdx.x < 32` rather than individual thread properties like parity) [S2, p. 46].\n\n**Sources:**\n*   [S1] Page 44\n*   [S2] Page 46\n*   [S3] Page 45",
